insert into question_pyq_meta (question_id, appear_count, years, exams, verified)
select id, appear_count, coalesce((select array_agg(v::int) from jsonb_array_elements_text(years) as v), '{}')::int[], coalesce((select array_agg(v) from jsonb_array_elements_text(exams) as v), '{}')::text[], verified
from jsonb_to_recordset($json$[{"id":"che-st-101","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Lavoisier’s law of conservation of mass states that, in a closed chemical change, the total mass of reactants compared with products is","options":["always greater","always smaller","exactly the same","unrelated"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Mass is neither created nor destroyed in ordinary chemical reactions, so reactant and product masses match.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-102","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Proust’s law of definite proportions says that a given pure compound always contains the same elements combined in","options":["any convenient ratio","a fixed mass ratio","a ratio that depends on how it was prepared","equal numbers of grams only"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The elemental composition by mass of a pure compound is constant, independent of source.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-103","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Dalton’s law of multiple proportions applies when two elements form more than one compound. The masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other stand in","options":["a simple whole-number ratio","an irrational ratio","the ratio of their densities","a 1:1 mass ratio always"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Different compounds of the same two elements show small-integer mass ratios for one element at fixed mass of the other.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-104","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Gay-Lussac’s law of combining volumes concerns gases measured at the same temperature and pressure. Their reacting volumes are","options":["in simple whole-number ratios","always equal to one litre","inversely proportional to molar mass only","unrelated to the chemical equation"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Gaseous reactants and products combine or form in small integer volume ratios under identical T and P.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-105","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Avogadro’s hypothesis, used with Gay-Lussac’s volume data, states that equal volumes of gases at the same T and P contain","options":["equal numbers of molecules","equal masses of gas","equal numbers of atoms in every case","one mole only if the gas is monatomic"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Equal gas volumes at the same T and P hold the same number of molecules, which explains simple volume ratios.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-106","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The law of reciprocal proportions (Richter) compares how two elements A and B each combine with a fixed mass of a third element C. The A:B mass ratio so obtained is","options":["the same as, or a simple multiple of, the ratio in which A and B combine with each other","always 1:1 by mass","equal to their atomic numbers","independent of any compound of A with B"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Masses of A and B that combine with a fixed mass of C match the combining ratio of A with B, or a simple multiple of it.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-107","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"In a sealed tube, 12 g of carbon is burnt completely in 32 g of oxygen to give carbon dioxide only. The mass of $\\mathrm{CO_2}$ that must be collected is","options":["12 g","32 g","44 g","16 g"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Conservation of mass requires $12 + 32 = 44$ g of product when nothing else is formed.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-108","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Carbon forms two oxides. With 12 g of carbon, one oxide contains 16 g of oxygen and the other contains 32 g of oxygen. These oxygen masses illustrate multiple proportions because their ratio is","options":["1 : 2","2 : 3","3 : 4","1 : 1"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"For a fixed 12 g of carbon the oxygen masses 16 g and 32 g stand in the simple ratio 1 : 2.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-109","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"At the same T and P, two volumes of hydrogen gas combine with one volume of oxygen gas to give steam. The volume of steam formed, if measured under the same conditions, is","options":["1 volume","2 volumes","3 volumes","4 volumes"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Gay-Lussac’s ratio for $2\\mathrm{H_2} + \\mathrm{O_2} \\rightarrow 2\\mathrm{H_2O(g)}$ is 2 : 1 : 2 by volume.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-110","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Two oxides of copper are analysed. In the first, 6.35 g of copper is combined with 0.80 g of oxygen; in the second, 6.35 g of copper is combined with 1.60 g of oxygen. The oxygen masses that combine with the same mass of copper are in the ratio","options":["1 : 2","2 : 3","3 : 4","4 : 5"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Fixed 6.35 g Cu takes 0.80 g then 1.60 g of oxygen, a 1 : 2 ratio, which is Dalton’s multiple-proportion test.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"laws-combination","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
    id text,
    chapter_id text,
    subject_id text,
    stem text,
